    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d316">

                <pb facs="0488" n="230" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg230"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1530</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-09-01">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 1, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 5.18 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2730. Your 2038, August 30, and my 2723, 31st.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d316-f1"/>
                    Foreign Office informs me unofficially that as this matter is so closely
                    connected with <pb facs="0489" n="231" xml:id="pg231"/> whole question of
                    permits for shipping goods of German origin from neutral ports, now under
                    discussion by British Government and about which French Government is also being
                    consulted, they cannot give an answer concerning this point alone but hope to
                    make a statement as to whole matter at a later date.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
